    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">\"Once Upon a Time\"</span> by Gabriel Okara is a reflective poem in which a father speaks to his son about how adults have become insincere and hypocritical, and expresses his wish to return to the innocent sincerity of childhood.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Background of the Poet</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Gabriel Okara (1921–2019) was a pioneering Nigerian poet and novelist from the Niger Delta region, known for blending African oral traditions with modern English verse. He is celebrated as one of the earliest African poets to write in English while preserving an authentically African voice and rhythm.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Summary of the Poem</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The poem is structured as a monologue addressed by a father to his son. The father contrasts how people used to greet each other warmly and sincerely \"once upon a time\" with how modern adults now wear false smiles, shake hands without warmth, and say one thing while meaning another. He describes how he too has learned to wear different \"faces\" like costumes for different occasions — a \"homeface,\" an \"officeface,\" a \"streetface,\" and a \"hostface.\" By the end, he expresses a longing to unlearn this hypocrisy and relearn the innocent, genuine laughter of his son and of his own childhood.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Themes</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Theme</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Hypocrisy and insincerity</td><td>Modern social life is full of false, put-on politeness rather than genuine feeling</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Loss of innocence</td><td>Adulthood and \"civilization\" have corrupted the natural sincerity of childhood</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nostalgia</td><td>The speaker longs for the simple, honest past</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Self-awareness/redemption</td><td>The father recognizes his own hypocrisy and wants to change, teaching by learning from his child</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Poetic Devices</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Okara uses conversational, simple diction; a controlled, reflective tone; repetition (e.g., of \"laugh,\" \"face,\" \"once upon a time\") to emphasize contrast between past and present; imagery of masks/faces to symbolize false identities; and a first-person confessional voice that makes the poem feel intimate and honest.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A very common question asks you to explain the significance of the different \"faces\" the speaker describes. Remember each face represents a different social mask worn for a different context/audience.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> \"Once Upon a Time\" is a father's confession to his son about how he has become insincere and hypocritical like other adults, and his heartfelt wish to relearn the innocent sincerity of childhood. Its central themes are hypocrisy, lost innocence, nostalgia, and the hope for redemption.\n</div>\n  ",
