    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">\"Night\"</span> by Wole Soyinka is a reflective poem that explores the coming of darkness both as a natural, calming phenomenon and as a metaphor for danger, mystery, and the unknown.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Background of the Poet</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Wole Soyinka is a Nigerian playwright, poet, and essayist, and the first African to win the Nobel Prize in Literature (1986). His works often explore themes of political oppression, African identity, and the tension between tradition and modernity, expressed through rich, layered imagery.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Summary of the Poem</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The poem describes the gradual arrival of night, personifying darkness as it spreads and swallows up the light and familiar sights of day. The speaker reflects on the way night transforms the visible, known world into something mysterious and uncertain, evoking both a sense of peace/rest and an undercurrent of unease or danger associated with the unknown.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Themes</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Theme</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Nature's power</td><td>Night is portrayed as an unstoppable natural force that transforms the world</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Duality of light and dark</td><td>Darkness represents both rest/calm and danger/the unknown</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mystery and the unknown</td><td>Night obscures what was clear in daylight, creating uncertainty</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Human vulnerability</td><td>The onset of night reminds humans of their limits and fears</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Poetic Devices</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Soyinka uses personification (night as an active, spreading force), rich sensory imagery to depict the transition from day to night, contrast between light and darkness, and a controlled, reflective tone that captures both the beauty and unease of nightfall.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Examiners often ask students to explain the dual/contrasting nature of night in the poem — remember it represents BOTH peaceful rest AND an undercurrent of danger/mystery, not just one or the other.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> \"Night\" by Wole Soyinka personifies the arrival of darkness as a powerful natural force that transforms the visible world, exploring themes of nature's power, the duality of light and dark, mystery, and human vulnerability in the face of the unknown.\n</div>\n  ",
