    "topic": "African Poetry: \"Not My Business\" by Niyi Osundare",
    "topicCode": "SS2-LIT-03",
    "module": "African Poetry",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">\"Not My Business\"</span> by Niyi Osundare is a powerful poem about political oppression and the dangers of indifference, showing how silence and apathy toward injustice eventually catch up with everyone.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Background of the Poet</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Niyi Osundare is an acclaimed Nigerian poet, dramatist, and literary critic known for socially engaged poetry that critiques political corruption, oppression, and social injustice, often using accessible language and rhythms rooted in African oral tradition.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Summary of the Poem</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The poem is structured in stanzas, each describing a different victim of political persecution — Akanni is beaten for asking questions, Danladi is jailed without trial, and Chinwe is dismissed from her job — with the speaker each time repeating that this is \"not my business\" and choosing to stay silent and unaffected. In the final stanza, the persecution comes directly to the speaker's own door, revealing the danger of remaining passive while injustice happens to others.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Themes</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Theme</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Political oppression</td><td>The poem depicts arbitrary persecution, arrest, and dismissal under an unjust regime</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Indifference and apathy</td><td>The speaker's repeated refusal to get involved reflects a common but dangerous attitude</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Collective responsibility</td><td>The poem argues that injustice against one person ultimately threatens everyone</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Self-preservation vs. solidarity</td><td>Choosing personal safety over standing up for others ultimately fails to protect anyone</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Poetic Devices</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Osundare uses a repeated refrain (\"Not my business\") to emphasize the theme of willful indifference; simple, direct, conversational language accessible to a wide audience; a structured, escalating stanza pattern building toward the ironic twist ending; and irony, since the speaker's own safety is ultimately destroyed by the very indifference meant to protect it.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Examiners frequently ask about the significance of the twist/final stanza. Remember: the poem's message is that indifference to injustice does NOT guarantee safety — it eventually reaches everyone.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> \"Not My Business\" tells of political persecution against several individuals while the speaker repeatedly claims indifference, only to have the same oppression reach his own door in the end. Its themes are political oppression, apathy, collective responsibility, and the danger of self-preserving silence.\n</div>\n  ",
