    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic covers two contrasting African poems: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">\"Hearty Garlands\"</span>, a celebratory tribute to old age and achievement, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">\"New Tongue\"</span>, a lament over the loss of African cultural values to Western influence.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. \"Hearty Garlands\" by S.O.H. Afriyie-Vidza</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A Ghanaian poet, Afriyie-Vidza writes this 35-line panegyric (praise poem) celebrating an individual who has reached eighty-five years of age. Written in the second person (\"you\"), the poem praises the celebrant's long life, wisdom, and achievements, treating old age as something to be honoured rather than feared, reflecting African cultural respect for elders.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. \"New Tongue\" by Elizabeth L. A. Kamara</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A Sierra Leonean poet and academic, Kamara's poem laments how the younger generation of Africans has abandoned traditional values, language, and respect for elders in favour of adopting Western (\"borrowed\") customs, dress, and attitudes. The poem mourns broken family ties and a growing culture of selfish individualism replacing communal African values.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Themes Compared</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Poem</th><th>Key Themes</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Hearty Garlands</td><td>Respect for age/wisdom, celebration of achievement, community appreciation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>New Tongue</td><td>Cultural loss, colonial influence, generational divide, individualism vs. communalism</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Poetic Devices</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Hearty Garlands</strong> uses second-person direct address, celebratory/reflective tone, and metaphors (e.g., life's process \"blooming\" into beauty) to convey a joyous tribute. <strong>New Tongue</strong> uses metaphor and symbolism (e.g., \"borrowed tongue,\" ties \"loosened, broken, burnt\"), vivid and evocative imagery of destruction, and repetition of phrases like \"No room for elders\" to reinforce the theme of cultural detachment.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> These two poems make a strong compare-and-contrast pair: one celebrates cultural continuity and respect for tradition (Hearty Garlands), while the other mourns its loss (New Tongue). Expect essay questions asking you to compare their treatment of African values.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> \"Hearty Garlands\" celebrates the wisdom and achievement of old age within African tradition, while \"New Tongue\" laments the abandonment of that same tradition by a Westernized younger generation — together illustrating contrasting perspectives on African cultural heritage.\n</div>\n  ",
