    "topic": "Non-African Poetry: \"Still I Rise\" by Maya Angelou",
    "topicCode": "SS2-LIT-06",
    "module": "Non-African Poetry",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">\"Still I Rise\"</span> by Maya Angelou is a powerful, defiant poem celebrating resilience, self-worth, and the unbreakable spirit of Black people in the face of oppression, racism, and hardship.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Background of the Poet</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Maya Angelou (1928–2014) was an American poet, memoirist, and civil rights activist. Her work often addresses themes of racial identity, resilience, and empowerment, drawing on her own experiences of hardship and discrimination in the United States.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Summary of the Poem</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The speaker directly addresses an oppressor who has tried to write her down in history with \"bitter, twisted lies\" and tread her \"in the very dirt.\" Despite this, she declares repeatedly that she will \"rise\" — asserting her confidence, sensuality, pride, and unbreakable spirit. The poem moves through the speaker's defiant response to attempts at humiliation, celebrating her own self-assurance and looking forward to a future shaped by the resilience of her ancestors, rising like \"a black ocean\" out of the \"huts of history's shame.\"</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Themes</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Theme</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Resilience and perseverance</td><td>The refusal to be broken by oppression, discrimination, or hardship</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Self-confidence and pride</td><td>Unapologetic celebration of the speaker's identity, body, and spirit</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Racial oppression</td><td>References to slavery and historical mistreatment of Black people</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Hope and empowerment</td><td>A forward-looking vision of overcoming hardship and inspiring future generations</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Poetic Devices</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Angelou uses a powerful refrain (\"I rise\") to build momentum and defiance; rhetorical questions directed at the oppressor; vivid, confident imagery (comparing herself to dust, moons, tides, and oceans); rhythmic, almost song-like structure evoking spoken-word and African American oral traditions; and a triumphant, ascending tone that builds toward the poem's climactic ending.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Focus on the poem's use of natural imagery (dust, moons, tides, oceans) as symbols of unstoppable, cyclical resilience — a frequently tested aspect of the poem's poetic technique.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> \"Still I Rise\" is a defiant, empowering poem in which the speaker repeatedly asserts her resilience against oppression and humiliation, celebrating self-confidence, pride, and hope for a future built on the unbreakable spirit of her ancestors.\n</div>\n  ",
