    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">\"She Walks in Beauty\"</span> by Lord Byron is a classic Romantic love poem celebrating a woman's perfect union of outer physical beauty and inner moral goodness, expressed through imagery of light and darkness in harmony.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Background of the Poet</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">George Gordon, Lord Byron (1788–1824), was a leading English Romantic poet known for his passionate, lyrical verse and larger-than-life persona. \"She Walks in Beauty\" is one of his most celebrated short lyric poems, written after seeing his cousin's wife at a party wearing a black mourning dress with sparkling ornaments.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Summary of the Poem</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The poem describes a woman whose beauty combines the best of both darkness and brightness, like a \"cloudless night\" full of stars. Her external beauty (in face, eyes, and hair) is presented as being in perfect harmony with her inner qualities — purity, calm, innocence, and goodness of heart and mind. The poem argues that true beauty is not simply physical, but a reflection of inner peace and virtue.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Themes</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Theme</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Harmony of beauty</td><td>The balance between light and dark, physical beauty and inner virtue</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Inner vs. outer beauty</td><td>True beauty reflects goodness of mind and heart, not appearance alone</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Idealized femininity</td><td>The Romantic ideal of the perfect, virtuous, beautiful woman</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nature imagery</td><td>Comparing human beauty to natural phenomena like stars and night skies</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Poetic Devices</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Byron uses simile (comparing the woman to a \"cloudless night\" and starry skies), balanced antithesis (contrasting light and dark, \"dark and bright\"), regular rhyme and meter typical of Romantic lyric poetry, and gentle, harmonious imagery that mirrors the poem's central theme of balance and harmony.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A key exam focus is the poem's central idea that beauty is a HARMONY of opposites (light/dark, physical/spiritual) rather than one extreme — students should be ready to explain this balance with reference to specific imagery.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> \"She Walks in Beauty\" celebrates a woman whose external beauty perfectly reflects her inner purity and goodness, using imagery of light and darkness in harmony to convey the Romantic ideal that true beauty unites physical grace with virtue.\n</div>\n  ",
