    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic pairs two contrasting non-African poems: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">\"Digging\"</span>, a reflective tribute to family heritage and honest labour, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">\"The Telephone Call\"</span>, a satirical poem about deception and false hope.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. \"Digging\" by Seamus Heaney</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Seamus Heaney, an Irish Nobel laureate poet, reflects on his father and grandfather's skilled work digging potatoes and cutting turf on their family farm. As the speaker listens to his father digging outside his window, he reflects that he has \"no spade to follow men like them,\" but resolves that his pen will be his own equivalent tool — he will \"dig\" with writing just as they dug with spades, honouring their legacy through his own craft.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. \"The Telephone Call\" by Fleur Adcock</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Fleur Adcock's poem describes a phone call from a fictional company called \"Universal Lotteries\" informing the speaker she has won a huge prize. Her excitement builds through the conversation, but the callers reveal there is no actual cheque — the \"prize\" was merely the exciting experience of the call itself. The poem satirizes how scams and modern consumer culture manufacture false hope and manipulate emotions for no real reward.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Themes Compared</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Poem</th><th>Key Themes</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Digging</td><td>Family heritage, honest labour, the value of the writer's craft as a legitimate \"digging\"</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The Telephone Call</td><td>Deception, false hope, disillusionment, satire of consumer/scam culture</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Poetic Devices</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Digging</strong> uses extended metaphor (the pen as a spade), sensory imagery of physical labour (sound, smell, texture of digging), and a reflective, respectful tone. <strong>The Telephone Call</strong> uses dialogue/conversational structure, irony (a \"prize\" that turns out to be nothing), sarcasm, and a shift in tone from excitement to disappointment to convey its satirical message.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Note the contrast in tone: \"Digging\" is warmly reflective and respectful of real, honest labour, while \"The Telephone Call\" is satirical and critical of dishonest manipulation — a useful compare/contrast angle for essays.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> \"Digging\" honours family heritage and honest labour by comparing writing to physical digging, while \"The Telephone Call\" satirizes modern scams and false promises through an ironic, disappointing phone conversation.\n</div>\n  ",
