    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Redemption Road: The Quest for Peace and Justice in Liberia</span> by Elma Shaw is a powerful novel set in post-civil-war Liberia, following survivors as they confront trauma and seek healing and justice.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Background of the Author</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Elma Shaw is a Liberian author and humanitarian committed to peacebuilding and social justice. <em>Redemption Road</em>, first published in 2008, is her debut novel, drawing on her deep engagement with reconciliation and trauma healing following Liberia's devastating civil war (1989–2003).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Setting of the Novel</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The novel is set in Monrovia, Liberia, during the administration of President Charles Taylor, shortly after the brutal Liberian Civil War. The story moves between Monrovia and rural villages, contrasting the slow urban recovery with the devastation still visible in the countryside. The title references the historical \"Redemption Road\" in Monrovia, where political executions took place after the 1980 coup — a powerful symbol of Liberia's violent past and its ongoing search for healing.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Plot Structure</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The novel opens with the Lewis family commemorating the tenth anniversary of the death of their matriarch, Granny May, who died during the war. The narrative then follows protagonist Bendu Lewis, a young Americo-Liberian woman who counsels other war survivors while carrying her own painful war-time secrets. When she discovers that the warlord who once held her captive (Commander Cobra, now going by the name Moses Varney) is living freely in town, she resolves to pursue justice against him, setting the novel's central conflict in motion.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Structural Features</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Physical setting</td><td>Monrovia and rural Liberian villages, post-civil-war</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Social setting</td><td>A fractured society grappling with trauma, poverty, and the search for justice</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Temporal setting</td><td>The period following Liberia's civil war, under Charles Taylor's administration</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Central conflict</td><td>Bendu's pursuit of justice against her former captor, Commander Cobra</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the symbolic significance of the title: \"Redemption Road\" refers to a real historical site of executions in Monrovia, making the title itself a metaphor for Liberia's journey from violence toward healing.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Redemption Road is set in post-civil-war Monrovia and rural Liberia, following Bendu Lewis's pursuit of justice against her former captor. Its setting and structure powerfully evoke Liberia's collective trauma and its long path toward healing and reconciliation.\n</div>\n  ",
