    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic examines the major characters introduced in the early chapters of <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Redemption Road</span> and the key events that establish the novel's central conflict.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Major Characters</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Bendu Marie Lewis</td><td>Protagonist; young Americo-Liberian woman and war survivor who counsels others</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Moses Varney / Commander Cobra</td><td>Antagonist; former rebel commander who held Bendu captive during the war</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Catherine May Lewis (\"Granny May\")</td><td>Bendu's paternal grandmother, whose memorial opens the novel; died during the war</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Eva Lewis</td><td>Bendu's mother, who carries her own grief over the loss of her son Benji</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Siatta</td><td>Bendu's sister, who confronts the realities of post-war life and an unfaithful husband</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Agnes Jallah</td><td>Bendu's colleague who counsels war survivors alongside her</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Early Events (Chapters 1–5)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The novel opens with the Lewis family's tenth-anniversary memorial for Granny May, who died at Charlue Town during the war. Bendu, now working alongside Agnes counseling other war survivors, carries her own hidden war-time trauma. The early chapters reveal Bendu's history of captivity, including her brutal marriage to a man named Samson while held at a rebel camp called Duluma, and her decision to abandon the baby daughter she had there. When Bendu learns that her former captor Cobra has resurfaced in town under the name Moses Varney, she resolves to bring him to justice, despite Agnes's urging that she drop the case.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Character Relationships and Conflict</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The early chapters establish Bendu's inner conflict between the pursuit of justice and the pain of confronting her own painful secrets. Her relationship with her family (mother Eva, sister Siatta) reveals how differently each family member processes their war-time grief and trauma, setting up parallel storylines of healing throughout the novel.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to explain the significance of Moses Varney's dual identity (also known as Commander Cobra) — this reveal is central to the novel's plot and Bendu's motivation for seeking justice.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The early chapters of Redemption Road introduce Bendu Lewis and her family as they grapple with grief and hidden trauma, culminating in the revelation that Bendu's former captor Cobra now lives freely as Moses Varney — the event that sets the novel's central conflict in motion.\n</div>\n  ",
