    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Chapters 6–10 of <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Redemption Road</span> deepen the novel's exploration of justice, trauma, and reconciliation as Bendu's legal case against Moses Varney develops alongside her family's parallel healing journeys.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Major Themes</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Theme</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Justice vs. forgiveness</td><td>Bendu must weigh pursuing legal accountability against the possibility of forgiveness and moving on</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Trauma and healing</td><td>Different characters process and begin to heal from war-time trauma in different ways</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Family and resilience</td><td>The Lewis family's bonds are tested and strengthened through shared grief</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Secrets and disclosure</td><td>Characters grapple with whether and how to reveal painful war-time secrets</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Character Development</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">As the case against Moses Varney progresses, Bendu's character deepens — she is shown as increasingly torn between her desire for justice and the emotional cost of publicly confronting her past. Meanwhile, her mother Eva Lewis begins to open up about her long-carried grief over her son Benji, clarifying to her daughters that her devotion to his memory was never meant to suggest she loved him more than them. Bendu's sister Siatta also confronts the realities of her failing marriage to the unfaithful Terrance, moving toward the decision to divorce him.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Plot Developments</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Bendu reports Moses Varney to the police, but faces obstacles in prosecuting him given the chaotic post-war justice system. Agnes continues to urge caution, worried about the emotional and practical risks Bendu faces. These chapters build tension around whether Bendu will succeed in holding her former captor accountable, while deepening the reader's understanding of how trauma has shaped each member of the Lewis family differently.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A common essay question asks you to discuss how different characters (Bendu, Eva, Siatta) each represent different approaches to processing grief and trauma — study each character's individual journey carefully.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Chapters 6–10 deepen the novel's themes of justice, trauma, and family resilience as Bendu pursues her case against Moses Varney while her mother and sister confront their own grief and relationship struggles, illustrating the varied paths toward healing after war.\n</div>\n  ",
