    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">African drama</span> has deep roots in traditional oral performance, ritual, and storytelling, evolving over the 20th century into a rich body of written plays that blend indigenous cultural forms with Western theatrical traditions.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Origins of African Drama</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Long before written plays existed, African societies had rich traditions of performance — ritual ceremonies, festival masquerades, storytelling with music and dance, and oral narratives performed by griots and other custodians of tradition. These forms combined elements of drama, music, dance, and audience participation that continue to influence modern African playwrights.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Cultural Influences on African Drama</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Influence</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Oral tradition</td><td>Proverbs, folktales, songs, and communal storytelling shape structure and dialogue</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ritual and festival</td><td>Traditional ceremonies (masquerades, rites of passage) inform performance style</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Colonial encounter</td><td>Western theatrical forms and the English/French language were introduced through colonial education</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Post-colonial identity</td><td>Modern playwrights often address themes of independence, cultural conflict, and social justice</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Key Features of African Drama</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">African drama frequently features music, dance, and audience participation integrated into performance; use of proverbs and indigenous idiom in dialogue; exploration of the tension between tradition and modernity; strong moral or communal messages; and characters representing broader social types or values rather than purely individual psychology.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Notable African Playwrights</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Prominent African dramatists include Wole Soyinka (Nigeria, Nobel laureate), Efua Sutherland (Ghana), Ola Rotimi (Nigeria), and Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o (Kenya), among others, who have each contributed significantly to shaping African drama as both an artistic and a socio-political tool.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to explain how African drama differs from purely Western dramatic traditions — especially its roots in oral performance, communal participation, and the blending of music/dance/dialogue.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> African drama grew out of rich oral, ritual, and festival traditions, later blending with Western theatrical forms introduced through colonialism. It is characterized by the integration of music, dance, proverb-rich dialogue, and themes exploring tradition, modernity, and social justice.\n</div>\n  ",
