    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">The Marriage of Anansewa</span> by Efua Sutherland is a Ghanaian comedy of manners that follows the cunning trickster Ananse as he schemes to marry off his daughter to four wealthy chiefs simultaneously for financial gain.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Background of the Playwright</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Efua Sutherland (1924–1996) was a pioneering Ghanaian playwright, poet, and theatre practitioner who helped establish modern Ghanaian theatre. She drew heavily on the Anansesem (Ananse stories) — a rich Akan oral storytelling tradition centred on the trickster spider-man Ananse — adapting this folk tradition into modern dramatic form.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Setting</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The play is set in a contemporary Ghanaian household and draws its plot and characters from the well-known Ananse folktale tradition. Sutherland innovatively presents the play with \"mbguesem\" (proverb-linking) song interludes performed by a Storyteller and a chorus, situating the drama within a distinctly African performance style that blends traditional storytelling conventions with modern stagecraft.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Act One Summary</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Act One introduces Ananse, a cunning but financially struggling father, and his daughter Anansewa. Ananse has been secretly collecting \"engagement gifts\" (a form of bride price) from four different wealthy chiefs — Chief-of-Chiefs, Chief Kwame, Chief Tomo Kagbe, and Chief-in-Two-Countries — each of whom believes he is Anansewa's sole suitor. Ananse's clever scheming and constant excuses to keep each suitor waiting and unaware of the others form the comic backbone of the opening act, while his daughter Anansewa's true feelings and eventual fate remain uncertain.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Key Characters Introduced</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Ananse</td><td>The cunning, scheming father-protagonist, driven by financial need</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Anansewa</td><td>Ananse's daughter, whose marriage is being negotiated without her full input</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The four chiefs</td><td>Wealthy suitors, each unaware of the others' simultaneous courtship</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The Storyteller/Chorus</td><td>Narrates and comments on the action, in the style of traditional Akan storytelling</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember that Ananse is a classic trickster figure from Akan folklore — his cunning, scheming nature in the play directly reflects his traditional role in Ananse folktales, making this an important link between oral tradition and written drama.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The Marriage of Anansewa opens with Ananse secretly negotiating marriage arrangements for his daughter with four different wealthy chiefs simultaneously, driven by his financial troubles, blending Akan trickster folklore with modern comic drama and storytelling conventions.\n</div>\n  ",
