    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Beyond its comic plot, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">The Marriage of Anansewa</span> offers rich moral lessons about greed, honesty, and the balance between tradition and individual agency, delivered through Sutherland's distinctive dramatic style.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Moral Lessons</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Lesson</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Greed has consequences</td><td>Ananse's scheming, though ultimately forgiven, nearly leads to disaster and public humiliation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Honesty and integrity matter</td><td>The near-collapse of Ananse's lies reinforces the value of truthful dealing</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Marriage should not be purely transactional</td><td>Anansewa's eventual match with a genuinely worthy suitor affirms the value of compatibility over pure wealth</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Respect for young people's agency</td><td>The play subtly critiques arranging marriages without regard for the individual's own wishes</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Dramatic Style</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Sutherland's style blends comedy with social commentary, using humor to explore serious themes without becoming heavy-handed. The play's use of the Storyteller/Chorus device, drawn from oral tradition, allows for direct commentary on the action and creates a sense of shared communal storytelling between performers and audience.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Use of Language and Song</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The dialogue incorporates proverbs and culturally specific idiom, giving the play an authentically Ghanaian voice. Song and music interludes (mbguesem) are integrated throughout, not merely as decoration but as an active storytelling device that comments on and advances the plot, reflecting the play's roots in traditional Akan performance.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Broader Significance</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The Marriage of Anansewa remains a landmark of modern African drama for successfully merging entertainment with social critique, demonstrating how traditional oral forms could be adapted for the modern stage while still speaking meaningfully to contemporary audiences about universal human flaws like greed and self-interest.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> When discussing moral lessons, always connect them back to specific plot events (e.g., the fake death nearly failing) rather than stating themes in the abstract — examiners reward textual evidence.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The Marriage of Anansewa teaches moral lessons about the dangers of greed and the importance of honesty and genuine compatibility in marriage, delivered through Sutherland's distinctive style blending comedy, proverb-rich dialogue, and traditional Akan storytelling and song.\n</div>\n  ",
