    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">A Man for All Seasons</span> by Robert Bolt is a historical drama dramatizing the final years of Sir Thomas More, a man who refused to compromise his conscience even when it cost him everything, including his life.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Background of the Playwright</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Robert Bolt (1924–1995) was an English playwright and screenwriter, best known for A Man for All Seasons (1960), which won the Tony Award for Best Play and was later adapted into an Academy Award-winning film. Bolt was drawn to More's story as an example of individual conscience standing firm against the pressures of political power.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Historical Setting</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The play is set in 16th-century England during the reign of King Henry VIII, dramatizing the historical conflict between the King and Sir Thomas More, the Lord Chancellor. Henry VIII sought an annulment of his marriage to Catherine of Aragon in order to marry Anne Boleyn, breaking from the Roman Catholic Church to do so. More, a devout Catholic and man of principle, refused to support the King's actions, setting up the play's central conflict.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Act One and Two Summary</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The play traces More's rise as Lord Chancellor and his increasing political isolation as he refuses to endorse Henry VIII's divorce and remarriage. Bolt introduces the symbolic character of \"The Common Man,\" who narrates and comments on the action while playing multiple minor roles, representing ordinary people's pragmatic, self-preserving responses to political pressure — a stark contrast to More's unwavering principle.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Key Characters</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Sir Thomas More</td><td>Protagonist; Lord Chancellor who refuses to compromise his conscience</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>King Henry VIII</td><td>Demands support for his divorce and remarriage; grows hostile toward More</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The Common Man</td><td>A choric figure representing ordinary people's pragmatic self-interest</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Thomas Cromwell</td><td>Ambitious, unscrupulous politician who works to bring More down</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cardinal Wolsey</td><td>More's predecessor as Chancellor, whose fall foreshadows the political dangers ahead</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Understand the symbolic function of \"The Common Man\" — he represents the ordinary person's instinct for self-preservation, standing in direct thematic contrast to More's principled refusal to compromise.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> A Man for All Seasons dramatizes Sir Thomas More's refusal to support Henry VIII's divorce and remarriage on grounds of conscience, using the symbolic figure of The Common Man to contrast principled integrity with ordinary self-preservation.\n</div>\n  ",
