    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The remaining acts of <span class=\"learn-keyword\">A Man for All Seasons</span> bring More's principled resistance to its tragic conclusion, deepening the play's exploration of conscience, integrity, and the cost of standing firm against power.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Plot Resolution</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">As the play progresses, More resigns as Lord Chancellor rather than support the King, choosing careful legal silence rather than open defiance. However, when Parliament passes the Act of Succession requiring an oath recognizing Henry's new marriage and supremacy over the Church, More's continued refusal to swear the oath leads to his imprisonment in the Tower of London, a show trial built on perjured testimony (from Richard Rich), and ultimately his execution.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Major Themes</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Theme</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Conscience vs. political power</td><td>More's refusal to betray his beliefs even at the cost of his life</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Integrity and its cost</td><td>Standing firm on principle brings personal ruin, imprisonment, and death</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Silence as resistance</td><td>More's legal strategy of silence reflects his careful, principled resistance within the law</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Corruption and self-interest</td><td>Characters like Cromwell and Rich advance themselves through betrayal and moral compromise</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Characterization</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">More is portrayed as intelligent, principled, and even witty under pressure, using his legal training to try to protect himself without directly betraying his conscience. Richard Rich, a minor character, is portrayed as morally weak, ultimately perjuring himself against More in exchange for political advancement — a stark illustration of the corrupting influence of ambition, in direct contrast to More's integrity.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Significance of the Title</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The title \"A Man for All Seasons\" originally referred to More's reputation as someone who could be relied upon in any circumstance — a person of unwavering good character regardless of the pressures around him. The title thus captures the central thematic message of the play: the enduring value of moral consistency and integrity.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Understand More's strategy of legal silence as a form of resistance — he does not openly defy the King but relies on the principle that silence cannot be legally construed as treason, a nuance often tested in essay questions.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> A Man for All Seasons culminates in More's imprisonment and execution after his refusal to swear the Oath of Succession, exploring themes of conscience, integrity, and the corrupting cost of political ambition, particularly through the contrasting character of Richard Rich.\n</div>\n  ",
