    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">An Inspector Calls</span> by J.B. Priestley is a gripping mystery drama in which a wealthy family's involvement in the death of a young working-class woman is gradually exposed by a mysterious police inspector.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Background of the Playwright</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">J.B. Priestley (1894–1984) was an English novelist, playwright, and broadcaster. Written in 1945 but set in 1912, An Inspector Calls reflects Priestley's strong socialist convictions and his concern with social responsibility, class inequality, and the consequences of selfish individualism, themes shaped by his experiences of both World Wars.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Setting</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The play is set in the fictional English industrial town of Brumley in 1912 — just two years before World War I and shortly before the sinking of the Titanic, events the audience (watching in 1945) would know about but the complacent Birling family does not. This dramatic irony underscores the play's critique of the family's blind confidence in their own social order.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Plot Summary</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The wealthy Birling family is celebrating their daughter Sheila's engagement to Gerald Croft when Inspector Goole arrives, investigating the suicide of a young working-class woman, Eva Smith. Through his questioning, the Inspector reveals that each family member — Mr. Birling, Sheila, Gerald, Mrs. Birling, and their son Eric — had some connection to and responsibility for a chain of events that led to Eva's death, exposing the family's selfishness, hypocrisy, and moral failures one by one.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Character Analysis</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Connection to Eva Smith & Character Trait</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Mr. Arthur Birling</td><td>Fired Eva from his factory for organizing a strike for fair wages; remains unrepentant and self-important</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sheila Birling</td><td>Had Eva fired from a shop out of jealousy; shows genuine remorse and grows morally throughout the play</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Gerald Croft</td><td>Had an affair with Eva (under the name Daisy Renton); shows some guilt but ultimately tries to minimize his role</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mrs. Sybil Birling</td><td>Refused Eva charity assistance while pregnant and desperate; remains cold and unrepentant</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Eric Birling</td><td>Got Eva pregnant and stole money to support her; shows genuine guilt and remorse</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Inspector Goole</td><td>Mysterious investigator who may represent conscience, justice, or a supernatural moral force</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the crucial generational divide: the younger characters (Sheila, Eric) show genuine remorse and change, while the older generation (Mr. and Mrs. Birling) remain unrepentant — this contrast is central to Priestley's message about social responsibility.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> An Inspector Calls exposes the Birling family's collective responsibility for the death of Eva Smith through Inspector Goole's investigation, contrasting the older generation's unrepentant selfishness with the younger generation's capacity for genuine moral growth.\n</div>\n  ",
